(window.FAIAS_DOC=window.FAIAS_DOC||{})["evidence-pack"]={"html":"<h1>EVIDENCE_PACK.md — FAIAS Component 4 Mandatory Evidence Inventory</h1>\n<p><strong>Version:</strong> the release version in <code>README.md</code>; the signed manifest, <code>ownership/MANIFEST.sha256</code>, identifies the release.<br />\n<strong>Document Custodian:</strong> the maintainer<br />\n<strong>Component:</strong> Component 4 (Evidence Catalogue)</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Purpose</h2>\n<p>This document defines the mandatory evidence pack for FAIAS assurance engagements. Evidence is organised by category (Governance, Business, Model, Data, Security, Operations, Human Oversight) and mapped to:\n- <strong>Expected Evidence Level</strong> (L1–L5 per ASSURANCE_MODEL.md section 5)\n- <strong>Assurance Claims Supported</strong> (which core claims does this evidence substantiate?)\n- <strong>Tier Applicability</strong> (which AI Risk Tiers require this evidence per TIERING_MODEL.md?)</p>\n<p>Every FAIAS engagement produces an Evidence Catalogue (Component 4 deliverable) by collecting evidence per this pack template. The catalogue supports auditability, transparency, and regulatory confidence.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Evidence Categories &amp; Mandatory Items</h2>\n<h3>CATEGORY 1: GOVERNANCE</h3>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Evidence that AI system ownership, accountability, and governance framework are clearly defined.</p>\n<h4>1.1 AI Governance Policy</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Firm-wide AI governance policy defining roles (model owner, escalation owner, compliance owner), approval workflows, monitoring responsibilities, incident escalation</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (design document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"System owner is defined and accountable\"; \"Governance structure is established\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> All tiers (Tier 0+)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> AI Governance Policy v[X], Board Resolution on AI Framework, SMCR Accountability Statement</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.2 System Inventory &amp; Classification</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Formal inventory of AI systems in production, classified by Tier (per TIERING_MODEL.md), with business owner, model provider, approval date</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (operational record)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Governance covers all material systems\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> All tiers (Tier 0+)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> AI System Inventory Spreadsheet (updated quarterly), System Registration Form</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.3 Accountability Model &amp; SMCR</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> SMCR statement defining which exec is accountable for model risk governance; escalation path for material findings; delegation authority limits</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (governance document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Accountability is clear and unambiguous\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 2–3 (Tier 0–1 may use functional delegation)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> SMCR Statement, Accountability Matrix, Delegation Letter</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.4 Approval Records &amp; Sign-Off</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Documented approval for system to deploy (by sponsor, risk committee, regulatory function if required). Pre-deployment and post-condition-satisfaction approvals.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (signed record)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Deployment was approved by competent authority\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+ (Tier 0 may use attestation)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Board Paper, Risk Committee Minutes, Sponsor Sign-Off, Condition Satisfaction Letter</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.5 Model Risk Governance Policy</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Firm's model risk governance framework (per PRA SS1/23 where applicable; BCBS 239 where AI content feeds risk-data aggregation). Defines model types subject to governance, review frequency, escalation triggers.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (policy document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Model risk governance is proportionate and documented\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 2–3</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Model Risk Governance Policy, MRG Framework Document</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>CATEGORY 2: BUSINESS</h3>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Evidence that the business use case, customer impact, process integration, and regulatory obligations are clearly understood.</p>\n<h4>2.1 Use Case Assessment</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> One-pager or brief document describing: business problem, how AI solves it, customer segment affected, materiality of decision, decision flow (human approval required?)</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (business document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Deployment context is clear\"; \"Risk tier is appropriate\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> All tiers</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Use Case One-Pager, Business Requirements Document, Process Map</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.2 Customer Impact Assessment</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Documented assessment of impact on affected customers: eligibility, pricing, access to service, data privacy, fairness implications. Identifies vulnerable customer populations if any.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (design document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Customer impact is understood and mitigated\"; \"Vulnerable customers (if any) have additional safeguards\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+ (assesses whether Tier 1 vs 2 vs 3)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Customer Impact Assessment, Vulnerability Analysis, Fairness Review Checklist</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.3 Process Maps &amp; Decision Workflows</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Current-state and future-state process diagrams showing: where AI output feeds in, who reviews/approves, what happens on exception, audit trail captured</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (process documentation)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Human review/oversight is designed into workflow\"; \"Escalation pathways are clear\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> BPMN Process Diagram, Decision Tree, Workflow Specification</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.4 Regulatory Obligations Mapping</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Firm's assessment of which regulatory frameworks apply (FCA SYSC 4.1, PRA Model Risk, GDPR, Consumer Duty, AML, Market Conduct, EU AI Act, etc.). Mapped to each obligation.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (compliance analysis)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Regulatory obligations are identified and addressed\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 2+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Regulatory Mapping Spreadsheet, SYSC 4.1 Governance Checklist, GDPR Impact Assessment</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>CATEGORY 3: MODEL</h3>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Evidence of model design, training rigour, evaluation results, and limitations.</p>\n<h4>3.1 Model Card</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Technical documentation of model: type (LLM, classifier, regressor, agent), architecture, training data, evaluation metrics, known limitations, scope of intended use, version history</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (technical design) + L4 (evaluation results if published benchmark)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Model behaviour is understood\"; \"Limitations are documented\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+ (Tier 0 may use vendor description)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Model Card per Hugging Face standard, Technical Specification, Provider Documentation</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.2 Model Evaluation &amp; Validation Results</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Evaluation on held-out test data or benchmark: accuracy, precision/recall, fairness metrics, robustness testing, hallucination rates, confidence calibration. Benchmarked against published standards (if available).</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L4 (independent testing) or L3 (design review) if staged-only</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Model performs within acceptable tolerances\"; \"Hallucination rate is measured and controlled\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Test Results Report, Evaluation Spreadsheet, Benchmark Comparison Table, Fairness Analysis</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.3 Fine-Tuning Documentation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> If model is fine-tuned: training data (source, size, quality), fine-tuning process (hyperparameters, validation approach), before/after performance comparison</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (design) + L4 (validation results)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Fine-tuning is justified and validated\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+ (if fine-tuning used)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Fine-Tuning Report, Data Inventory, Performance Comparison</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.4 Model Limitations &amp; Failure Modes</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Documented list of known failure modes, edge cases, scenarios where model is unreliable or out-of-distribution. Tied to IRQ-MOD series (hallucination, calibration, robustness, etc.)</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (design document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Model limitations are known and mitigated\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Limitations Statement, Failure Mode Analysis, Out-of-Distribution Testing Report</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.5 Version Control &amp; Model Registry</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Version history of model (training date, source data version, performance metrics per version, deprecation records). Model registry showing current production version.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (operational record)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Model versions are tracked and managed\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Git logs, Model Registry Entry, Version Comparison Table</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>CATEGORY 4: DATA</h3>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Evidence of data quality, lineage, sourcing, and integrity controls.</p>\n<h4>4.1 Data Lineage &amp; Source Documentation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> For training data: source, collection method, date collected, geographic scope, volume. For inference data (e.g., RAG retrieval sources): source location, update frequency, access controls.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (data documentation)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Data sources are known and controlled\"; \"RAG data is fresh and authoritative\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+ (Tier 0 may have minimal data risk)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Data Dictionary, Source Registry, Data Sourcing Agreement</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.2 Data Quality Assessment</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Quality metrics: completeness (% non-null), accuracy (comparison to ground truth if available), timeliness (staleness of records), uniqueness (duplicates), consistency across sources. Thresholds for acceptable quality.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L3 (design review) + L4 (testing/validation)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Data quality is measured and within acceptable thresholds\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Data Quality Report, Metrics Dashboard, Quality SLA</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.3 Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> GDPR/Data Protection assessment: lawful basis for processing, data subject rights (access, portability, erasure), privacy-preserving controls, consent management if applicable</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (compliance document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Data processing is lawful and privacy-protective\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> All tiers (required for GDPR compliance)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> DPIA Report, Privacy Notice, Consent Form</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.4 Data Bias &amp; Fairness Analysis</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> For classification/scoring models: analysis of model performance across protected attributes (gender, race, age, etc.), disparate impact testing, fairness metrics (demographic parity, equalized odds, etc.)</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L3 (design review) + L4 (testing)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Model performance is equitable across population groups\"; \"Bias mitigation is in place\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 2+ (especially for decisions affecting customer eligibility/pricing)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Bias Testing Report, Fairness Metrics Table, Remediation Plan</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.5 Data Retention &amp; Archival</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Data retention policy: how long training/inference data is retained, archival procedures, deletion/purging procedures. Audit trail of data governance actions.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (policy document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Data lifecycle is managed per policy\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Data Retention Policy, Archival Log, Deletion Certificates</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>CATEGORY 5: SECURITY</h3>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Evidence of security controls, threat analysis, and resilience.</p>\n<h4>5.1 Threat Model &amp; Security Assessment</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Documented threat model: attack vectors specific to this AI system (prompt injection, model extraction, data poisoning, supply-chain attacks, API compromise, etc.). Severity/likelihood assessment. Mapped to IRQ-SEC series.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (design document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Threats are identified and mitigated\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Threat Model Diagram, STRIDE Analysis, Threat-to-IRQ Mapping</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.2 Prompt Injection &amp; Jailbreak Testing</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> For LLM systems: testing against prompt injection, jailbreak, and instruction-following attacks. Test cases, results, confidence level (★1–★5).</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L4 (independent testing) or L3 (proof-of-concept demo)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"System is resilient to prompt injection\"; \"LLM behaviour is controllable\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+ (if LLM used)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Injection Test Report, Jailbreak Testing Results, OWASP LLM Top 10 Assessment</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.3 Model Extraction &amp; Adversarial Testing</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Testing for model robustness: adversarial examples, membership inference, extraction attacks (if relevant to deployment context). Test results and mitigations.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L4 (independent testing) or L3 (design review)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Model is resilient to adversarial attack\"; \"Confidentiality of model weights is protected\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 2+ (Tier 1 may defer to design review)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Adversarial Testing Report, Robustness Metrics, Extraction Test Results</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.4 Supply Chain &amp; Third-Party Security</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Assessment of third-party vendors: model providers (OpenAI, Anthropic, etc.), infrastructure (cloud, container registry), data providers. SLA audit, security certifications (SOC 2, ISO 27001), incident history.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (vendor attestations, SLAs) + L3 (audit review)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Third-party vendors meet security standards\"; \"Supply-chain risks are mitigated\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Vendor Security Questionnaire, SLA Review, Audit Report, Incident History</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.5 Access Controls &amp; Authentication</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Documentation of who can access model, data, APIs: authentication mechanisms (API keys, OAuth, SSO), least-privilege enforcement, access logging, audit trail</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (design document) + L3 (configuration review)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Access is controlled and auditable\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Access Control Policy, API Key Rotation Logs, Audit Trail Sample</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>CATEGORY 6: OPERATIONS</h3>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Evidence of deployment, monitoring, incident management, and change control.</p>\n<h4>6.1 Deployment Plan &amp; Configuration</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Go-live plan: deployment date, rollback procedure, monitoring cutover, communication plan. Production system configuration (model endpoint, inference settings, rate limits).</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (operational plan)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Deployment is planned and controlled\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Deployment Checklist, Go-Live Plan, Rollback Procedure, Configuration Document</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.2 Monitoring &amp; Alerting Setup</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Operational KPIs defined: system latency, accuracy, hallucination rate, user satisfaction, error rates. Alert thresholds. Monitoring dashboard screenshots or link.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (design) + L3 (operational confirmation)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"System behaviour is continuously monitored\"; \"Anomalies trigger escalation\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> KPI Definition Sheet, Dashboard Screenshots, Alert Configuration, Alert Trigger Examples</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.3 Incident Management &amp; Escalation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Incident management procedure: how incidents are detected, reported, investigated, escalated. Incident log (last 12 months) showing incidents, root cause, remediation, lessons learned.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (procedure) + L3 (incident records)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Incidents are managed and escalated appropriately\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Incident Management Policy, Incident Log, Post-Incident Review, Escalation Matrix</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.4 Change Management &amp; Update Procedure</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Procedure for model updates, prompt changes, data source changes, parameter tuning: change request process, approvals, testing, rollback. Change log for last 12 months.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (procedure) + L3 (change log)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Changes are controlled and tested\"; \"Rollback capability is maintained\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Change Management Policy, Change Log, Testing Evidence per Change, Approval Records</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.5 Backup &amp; Disaster Recovery</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Backup strategy for model weights, configuration, data. Recovery time objective (RTO), recovery point objective (RPO). Tested recovery procedure.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (design) + L3 (test evidence)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"System can recover from failure within acceptable timeframe\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 2+ (Tier 1 may use vendor SLA)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Disaster Recovery Plan, Backup Log, Recovery Test Report</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>CATEGORY 7: HUMAN OVERSIGHT</h3>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Evidence that humans can understand, review, and override AI decisions.</p>\n<h4>7.1 Explainability &amp; Interpretability</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> For black-box models: explanation methods (LIME, SHAP, attention weights, etc.) and example explanations. For transparent models: feature importance, rule lists. User-facing explanation templates.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L3 (design review + demo) or L4 (independent testing if explanation quality matters)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"System outputs are explainable to human reviewers\"; \"Reviewers can understand why system made a decision\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+ (especially Tier 2–3 for material decisions)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Explanation Method Documentation, Example Explanations, User Interface Screenshots</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.2 Escalation &amp; Override Process</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Escalation procedure: which scenarios trigger escalation? Who decides? SLA for response? Override procedure: can humans reject AI recommendation? Logged and audited?</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (procedure design) + L3 (procedure evidence, override logs)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Humans can escalate or override without friction\"; \"Escalations are rare (non-adversarial)\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Escalation Procedure, Override Log, SLA Evidence, Training Records</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.3 Reviewer Training &amp; Competence</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Training program for human reviewers: what is AI, how to interpret outputs, when to escalate, what to watch for. Training records, competence assessment results.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (training materials) + L3 (training records, test results)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Reviewers are trained and competent\"; \"Alert fatigue is managed via training\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Training Slides, Training Record, Competence Assessment Test, Remediation Records</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.4 Override Analysis &amp; Alert Fatigue</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Operational analysis of override patterns: % of recommendations overridden, reasons for override, trends over time. Alert fatigue indicator: if override rate &gt;15%, escalate.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L3 (operational analysis) + L4 (if auditor validates)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Override rates are normal (not indicating automation bias)\"; \"Alert fatigue is not a risk\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+ (especially after early-life monitoring)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Override Analysis Report, Trend Charts, Alert Fatigue Assessment</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.5 Accountability &amp; Contestability</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Documentation of accountability: who is responsible for a decision? AI or human? Audit trail showing who reviewed and approved/rejected. Customer redress procedure if decision was wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (procedure) + L3 (audit trail samples)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Customer accountability is clear\"; \"Customers can contest decisions\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 2–3 (especially for autonomous decisions)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Accountability Matrix, Audit Trail Sample, Escalation Procedure, Customer Redress Policy</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h2>Evidence Pack Assembly by Tier</h2>\n<h3>Tier 0: Light-Touch Assessment</h3>\n<p><strong>Required Evidence (minimum):</strong>\n- 1.2 System Inventory (Tier 0 entry)\n- 1.3 Accountability Model (attestation sufficient)\n- 2.1 Use Case Assessment (brief)\n- 3.1 Model Card (vendor description acceptable)</p>\n<p><strong>Expected Evidence Level:</strong> L2\n<strong>Engagement Duration:</strong> 1–2 weeks</p>\n<h3>Tier 1: Operational Support</h3>\n<p><strong>Required Evidence (all of below):</strong>\n- Category 1: 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.4\n- Category 2: 2.1, 2.2, 2.3\n- Category 3: 3.1, 3.2, 3.4\n- Category 4: 4.1, 4.2, 4.5\n- Category 5: 5.1, 5.4\n- Category 6: 6.1, 6.2, 6.3\n- Category 7: 7.1, 7.2, 7.3</p>\n<p><strong>Expected Evidence Level:</strong> L3–L4\n<strong>Engagement Duration:</strong> 4–6 weeks</p>\n<h3>Tier 2: Material Decision Support</h3>\n<p><strong>Required Evidence (all of below):</strong>\n- Category 1: 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.4, 1.5\n- Category 2: 2.1, 2.2, 2.3, 2.4\n- Category 3: 3.1, 3.2, 3.3, 3.4, 3.5\n- Category 4: 4.1, 4.2, 4.3, 4.4, 4.5\n- Category 5: 5.1, 5.2, 5.3, 5.4, 5.5\n- Category 6: 6.1, 6.2, 6.3, 6.4, 6.5\n- Category 7: 7.1, 7.2, 7.3, 7.4, 7.5</p>\n<p><strong>Expected Evidence Level:</strong> L4 (primary) + L3 (supporting)\n<strong>Engagement Duration:</strong> 8–12 weeks</p>\n<h3>Tier 3: Autonomous Material Decisioning</h3>\n<p><strong>Required Evidence (all of Tier 2, plus):</strong>\n- Post-deployment operational evidence (L5): monitoring data weeks 1–12, early-life findings, KPI performance\n- External audit of assessment methodology (per OPINION_TEMPLATE.md)\n- Annual operational validation: actual system performance in production against predicted performance</p>\n<p><strong>Expected Evidence Level:</strong> L4–L5\n<strong>Engagement Duration:</strong> 12–16 weeks + 12-month post-deployment validation</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Evidence Catalogue Management</h2>\n<p><strong>Tools &amp; Systems:</strong>\n- Evidence repository: shared folder with access controls, version control, retention policy\n- Evidence tracking spreadsheet: category, item, status (collected/pending/gap), evidence level, tier applicability, claim mapping\n- Audit trail: document who accessed what evidence, when; maintains chain of custody for regulatory requirements</p>\n<p><strong>Annual Refresh:</strong>\nEvery December, review evidence catalogue to identify gaps or expired documentation. Update as systems evolve (new data source, model update, process change).</p>\n<hr />","sections":[{"h":null,"html":"<h1>EVIDENCE_PACK.md — FAIAS Component 4 Mandatory Evidence Inventory</h1>\n<p><strong>Version:</strong> the release version in <code>README.md</code>; the signed manifest, <code>ownership/MANIFEST.sha256</code>, identifies the release.<br />\n<strong>Document Custodian:</strong> the maintainer<br />\n<strong>Component:</strong> Component 4 (Evidence Catalogue)</p>\n<hr />"},{"h":"Purpose","html":"<p>This document defines the mandatory evidence pack for FAIAS assurance engagements. Evidence is organised by category (Governance, Business, Model, Data, Security, Operations, Human Oversight) and mapped to:\n- <strong>Expected Evidence Level</strong> (L1–L5 per ASSURANCE_MODEL.md section 5)\n- <strong>Assurance Claims Supported</strong> (which core claims does this evidence substantiate?)\n- <strong>Tier Applicability</strong> (which AI Risk Tiers require this evidence per TIERING_MODEL.md?)</p>\n<p>Every FAIAS engagement produces an Evidence Catalogue (Component 4 deliverable) by collecting evidence per this pack template. The catalogue supports auditability, transparency, and regulatory confidence.</p>\n<hr />"},{"h":"Evidence Categories & Mandatory Items","html":"<h3>CATEGORY 1: GOVERNANCE</h3>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Evidence that AI system ownership, accountability, and governance framework are clearly defined.</p>\n<h4>1.1 AI Governance Policy</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Firm-wide AI governance policy defining roles (model owner, escalation owner, compliance owner), approval workflows, monitoring responsibilities, incident escalation</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (design document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"System owner is defined and accountable\"; \"Governance structure is established\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> All tiers (Tier 0+)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> AI Governance Policy v[X], Board Resolution on AI Framework, SMCR Accountability Statement</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.2 System Inventory &amp; Classification</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Formal inventory of AI systems in production, classified by Tier (per TIERING_MODEL.md), with business owner, model provider, approval date</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (operational record)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Governance covers all material systems\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> All tiers (Tier 0+)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> AI System Inventory Spreadsheet (updated quarterly), System Registration Form</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.3 Accountability Model &amp; SMCR</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> SMCR statement defining which exec is accountable for model risk governance; escalation path for material findings; delegation authority limits</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (governance document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Accountability is clear and unambiguous\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 2–3 (Tier 0–1 may use functional delegation)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> SMCR Statement, Accountability Matrix, Delegation Letter</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.4 Approval Records &amp; Sign-Off</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Documented approval for system to deploy (by sponsor, risk committee, regulatory function if required). Pre-deployment and post-condition-satisfaction approvals.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (signed record)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Deployment was approved by competent authority\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+ (Tier 0 may use attestation)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Board Paper, Risk Committee Minutes, Sponsor Sign-Off, Condition Satisfaction Letter</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.5 Model Risk Governance Policy</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Firm's model risk governance framework (per PRA SS1/23 where applicable; BCBS 239 where AI content feeds risk-data aggregation). Defines model types subject to governance, review frequency, escalation triggers.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (policy document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Model risk governance is proportionate and documented\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 2–3</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Model Risk Governance Policy, MRG Framework Document</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>CATEGORY 2: BUSINESS</h3>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Evidence that the business use case, customer impact, process integration, and regulatory obligations are clearly understood.</p>\n<h4>2.1 Use Case Assessment</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> One-pager or brief document describing: business problem, how AI solves it, customer segment affected, materiality of decision, decision flow (human approval required?)</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (business document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Deployment context is clear\"; \"Risk tier is appropriate\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> All tiers</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Use Case One-Pager, Business Requirements Document, Process Map</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.2 Customer Impact Assessment</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Documented assessment of impact on affected customers: eligibility, pricing, access to service, data privacy, fairness implications. Identifies vulnerable customer populations if any.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (design document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Customer impact is understood and mitigated\"; \"Vulnerable customers (if any) have additional safeguards\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+ (assesses whether Tier 1 vs 2 vs 3)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Customer Impact Assessment, Vulnerability Analysis, Fairness Review Checklist</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.3 Process Maps &amp; Decision Workflows</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Current-state and future-state process diagrams showing: where AI output feeds in, who reviews/approves, what happens on exception, audit trail captured</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (process documentation)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Human review/oversight is designed into workflow\"; \"Escalation pathways are clear\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> BPMN Process Diagram, Decision Tree, Workflow Specification</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.4 Regulatory Obligations Mapping</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Firm's assessment of which regulatory frameworks apply (FCA SYSC 4.1, PRA Model Risk, GDPR, Consumer Duty, AML, Market Conduct, EU AI Act, etc.). Mapped to each obligation.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (compliance analysis)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Regulatory obligations are identified and addressed\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 2+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Regulatory Mapping Spreadsheet, SYSC 4.1 Governance Checklist, GDPR Impact Assessment</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>CATEGORY 3: MODEL</h3>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Evidence of model design, training rigour, evaluation results, and limitations.</p>\n<h4>3.1 Model Card</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Technical documentation of model: type (LLM, classifier, regressor, agent), architecture, training data, evaluation metrics, known limitations, scope of intended use, version history</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (technical design) + L4 (evaluation results if published benchmark)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Model behaviour is understood\"; \"Limitations are documented\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+ (Tier 0 may use vendor description)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Model Card per Hugging Face standard, Technical Specification, Provider Documentation</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.2 Model Evaluation &amp; Validation Results</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Evaluation on held-out test data or benchmark: accuracy, precision/recall, fairness metrics, robustness testing, hallucination rates, confidence calibration. Benchmarked against published standards (if available).</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L4 (independent testing) or L3 (design review) if staged-only</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Model performs within acceptable tolerances\"; \"Hallucination rate is measured and controlled\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Test Results Report, Evaluation Spreadsheet, Benchmark Comparison Table, Fairness Analysis</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.3 Fine-Tuning Documentation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> If model is fine-tuned: training data (source, size, quality), fine-tuning process (hyperparameters, validation approach), before/after performance comparison</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (design) + L4 (validation results)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Fine-tuning is justified and validated\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+ (if fine-tuning used)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Fine-Tuning Report, Data Inventory, Performance Comparison</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.4 Model Limitations &amp; Failure Modes</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Documented list of known failure modes, edge cases, scenarios where model is unreliable or out-of-distribution. Tied to IRQ-MOD series (hallucination, calibration, robustness, etc.)</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (design document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Model limitations are known and mitigated\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Limitations Statement, Failure Mode Analysis, Out-of-Distribution Testing Report</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.5 Version Control &amp; Model Registry</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Version history of model (training date, source data version, performance metrics per version, deprecation records). Model registry showing current production version.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (operational record)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Model versions are tracked and managed\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Git logs, Model Registry Entry, Version Comparison Table</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>CATEGORY 4: DATA</h3>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Evidence of data quality, lineage, sourcing, and integrity controls.</p>\n<h4>4.1 Data Lineage &amp; Source Documentation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> For training data: source, collection method, date collected, geographic scope, volume. For inference data (e.g., RAG retrieval sources): source location, update frequency, access controls.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (data documentation)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Data sources are known and controlled\"; \"RAG data is fresh and authoritative\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+ (Tier 0 may have minimal data risk)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Data Dictionary, Source Registry, Data Sourcing Agreement</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.2 Data Quality Assessment</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Quality metrics: completeness (% non-null), accuracy (comparison to ground truth if available), timeliness (staleness of records), uniqueness (duplicates), consistency across sources. Thresholds for acceptable quality.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L3 (design review) + L4 (testing/validation)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Data quality is measured and within acceptable thresholds\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Data Quality Report, Metrics Dashboard, Quality SLA</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.3 Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> GDPR/Data Protection assessment: lawful basis for processing, data subject rights (access, portability, erasure), privacy-preserving controls, consent management if applicable</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (compliance document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Data processing is lawful and privacy-protective\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> All tiers (required for GDPR compliance)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> DPIA Report, Privacy Notice, Consent Form</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.4 Data Bias &amp; Fairness Analysis</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> For classification/scoring models: analysis of model performance across protected attributes (gender, race, age, etc.), disparate impact testing, fairness metrics (demographic parity, equalized odds, etc.)</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L3 (design review) + L4 (testing)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Model performance is equitable across population groups\"; \"Bias mitigation is in place\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 2+ (especially for decisions affecting customer eligibility/pricing)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Bias Testing Report, Fairness Metrics Table, Remediation Plan</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.5 Data Retention &amp; Archival</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Data retention policy: how long training/inference data is retained, archival procedures, deletion/purging procedures. Audit trail of data governance actions.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (policy document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Data lifecycle is managed per policy\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Data Retention Policy, Archival Log, Deletion Certificates</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>CATEGORY 5: SECURITY</h3>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Evidence of security controls, threat analysis, and resilience.</p>\n<h4>5.1 Threat Model &amp; Security Assessment</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Documented threat model: attack vectors specific to this AI system (prompt injection, model extraction, data poisoning, supply-chain attacks, API compromise, etc.). Severity/likelihood assessment. Mapped to IRQ-SEC series.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (design document)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Threats are identified and mitigated\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Threat Model Diagram, STRIDE Analysis, Threat-to-IRQ Mapping</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.2 Prompt Injection &amp; Jailbreak Testing</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> For LLM systems: testing against prompt injection, jailbreak, and instruction-following attacks. Test cases, results, confidence level (★1–★5).</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L4 (independent testing) or L3 (proof-of-concept demo)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"System is resilient to prompt injection\"; \"LLM behaviour is controllable\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+ (if LLM used)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Injection Test Report, Jailbreak Testing Results, OWASP LLM Top 10 Assessment</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.3 Model Extraction &amp; Adversarial Testing</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Testing for model robustness: adversarial examples, membership inference, extraction attacks (if relevant to deployment context). Test results and mitigations.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L4 (independent testing) or L3 (design review)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Model is resilient to adversarial attack\"; \"Confidentiality of model weights is protected\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 2+ (Tier 1 may defer to design review)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Adversarial Testing Report, Robustness Metrics, Extraction Test Results</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.4 Supply Chain &amp; Third-Party Security</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Assessment of third-party vendors: model providers (OpenAI, Anthropic, etc.), infrastructure (cloud, container registry), data providers. SLA audit, security certifications (SOC 2, ISO 27001), incident history.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (vendor attestations, SLAs) + L3 (audit review)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Third-party vendors meet security standards\"; \"Supply-chain risks are mitigated\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Vendor Security Questionnaire, SLA Review, Audit Report, Incident History</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.5 Access Controls &amp; Authentication</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Documentation of who can access model, data, APIs: authentication mechanisms (API keys, OAuth, SSO), least-privilege enforcement, access logging, audit trail</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (design document) + L3 (configuration review)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Access is controlled and auditable\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Access Control Policy, API Key Rotation Logs, Audit Trail Sample</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>CATEGORY 6: OPERATIONS</h3>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Evidence of deployment, monitoring, incident management, and change control.</p>\n<h4>6.1 Deployment Plan &amp; Configuration</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Go-live plan: deployment date, rollback procedure, monitoring cutover, communication plan. Production system configuration (model endpoint, inference settings, rate limits).</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (operational plan)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Deployment is planned and controlled\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Deployment Checklist, Go-Live Plan, Rollback Procedure, Configuration Document</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.2 Monitoring &amp; Alerting Setup</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Operational KPIs defined: system latency, accuracy, hallucination rate, user satisfaction, error rates. Alert thresholds. Monitoring dashboard screenshots or link.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (design) + L3 (operational confirmation)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"System behaviour is continuously monitored\"; \"Anomalies trigger escalation\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> KPI Definition Sheet, Dashboard Screenshots, Alert Configuration, Alert Trigger Examples</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.3 Incident Management &amp; Escalation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Incident management procedure: how incidents are detected, reported, investigated, escalated. Incident log (last 12 months) showing incidents, root cause, remediation, lessons learned.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (procedure) + L3 (incident records)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Incidents are managed and escalated appropriately\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Incident Management Policy, Incident Log, Post-Incident Review, Escalation Matrix</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.4 Change Management &amp; Update Procedure</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Procedure for model updates, prompt changes, data source changes, parameter tuning: change request process, approvals, testing, rollback. Change log for last 12 months.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (procedure) + L3 (change log)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Changes are controlled and tested\"; \"Rollback capability is maintained\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Change Management Policy, Change Log, Testing Evidence per Change, Approval Records</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.5 Backup &amp; Disaster Recovery</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Backup strategy for model weights, configuration, data. Recovery time objective (RTO), recovery point objective (RPO). Tested recovery procedure.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (design) + L3 (test evidence)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"System can recover from failure within acceptable timeframe\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 2+ (Tier 1 may use vendor SLA)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Disaster Recovery Plan, Backup Log, Recovery Test Report</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>CATEGORY 7: HUMAN OVERSIGHT</h3>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Evidence that humans can understand, review, and override AI decisions.</p>\n<h4>7.1 Explainability &amp; Interpretability</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> For black-box models: explanation methods (LIME, SHAP, attention weights, etc.) and example explanations. For transparent models: feature importance, rule lists. User-facing explanation templates.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L3 (design review + demo) or L4 (independent testing if explanation quality matters)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"System outputs are explainable to human reviewers\"; \"Reviewers can understand why system made a decision\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+ (especially Tier 2–3 for material decisions)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Explanation Method Documentation, Example Explanations, User Interface Screenshots</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.2 Escalation &amp; Override Process</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Escalation procedure: which scenarios trigger escalation? Who decides? SLA for response? Override procedure: can humans reject AI recommendation? Logged and audited?</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (procedure design) + L3 (procedure evidence, override logs)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Humans can escalate or override without friction\"; \"Escalations are rare (non-adversarial)\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Escalation Procedure, Override Log, SLA Evidence, Training Records</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.3 Reviewer Training &amp; Competence</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Training program for human reviewers: what is AI, how to interpret outputs, when to escalate, what to watch for. Training records, competence assessment results.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (training materials) + L3 (training records, test results)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Reviewers are trained and competent\"; \"Alert fatigue is managed via training\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Training Slides, Training Record, Competence Assessment Test, Remediation Records</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.4 Override Analysis &amp; Alert Fatigue</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Operational analysis of override patterns: % of recommendations overridden, reasons for override, trends over time. Alert fatigue indicator: if override rate &gt;15%, escalate.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L3 (operational analysis) + L4 (if auditor validates)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Override rates are normal (not indicating automation bias)\"; \"Alert fatigue is not a risk\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 1+ (especially after early-life monitoring)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Override Analysis Report, Trend Charts, Alert Fatigue Assessment</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.5 Accountability &amp; Contestability</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Documentation of accountability: who is responsible for a decision? AI or human? Audit trail showing who reviewed and approved/rejected. Customer redress procedure if decision was wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>Evidence Level:</strong> L2 (procedure) + L3 (audit trail samples)</li>\n<li><strong>Supporting Claims:</strong> \"Customer accountability is clear\"; \"Customers can contest decisions\"</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Applicability:</strong> Tier 2–3 (especially for autonomous decisions)</li>\n<li><strong>Example Artefacts:</strong> Accountability Matrix, Audit Trail Sample, Escalation Procedure, Customer Redress Policy</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />"},{"h":"Evidence Pack Assembly by Tier","html":"<h3>Tier 0: Light-Touch Assessment</h3>\n<p><strong>Required Evidence (minimum):</strong>\n- 1.2 System Inventory (Tier 0 entry)\n- 1.3 Accountability Model (attestation sufficient)\n- 2.1 Use Case Assessment (brief)\n- 3.1 Model Card (vendor description acceptable)</p>\n<p><strong>Expected Evidence Level:</strong> L2\n<strong>Engagement Duration:</strong> 1–2 weeks</p>\n<h3>Tier 1: Operational Support</h3>\n<p><strong>Required Evidence (all of below):</strong>\n- Category 1: 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.4\n- Category 2: 2.1, 2.2, 2.3\n- Category 3: 3.1, 3.2, 3.4\n- Category 4: 4.1, 4.2, 4.5\n- Category 5: 5.1, 5.4\n- Category 6: 6.1, 6.2, 6.3\n- Category 7: 7.1, 7.2, 7.3</p>\n<p><strong>Expected Evidence Level:</strong> L3–L4\n<strong>Engagement Duration:</strong> 4–6 weeks</p>\n<h3>Tier 2: Material Decision Support</h3>\n<p><strong>Required Evidence (all of below):</strong>\n- Category 1: 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.4, 1.5\n- Category 2: 2.1, 2.2, 2.3, 2.4\n- Category 3: 3.1, 3.2, 3.3, 3.4, 3.5\n- Category 4: 4.1, 4.2, 4.3, 4.4, 4.5\n- Category 5: 5.1, 5.2, 5.3, 5.4, 5.5\n- Category 6: 6.1, 6.2, 6.3, 6.4, 6.5\n- Category 7: 7.1, 7.2, 7.3, 7.4, 7.5</p>\n<p><strong>Expected Evidence Level:</strong> L4 (primary) + L3 (supporting)\n<strong>Engagement Duration:</strong> 8–12 weeks</p>\n<h3>Tier 3: Autonomous Material Decisioning</h3>\n<p><strong>Required Evidence (all of Tier 2, plus):</strong>\n- Post-deployment operational evidence (L5): monitoring data weeks 1–12, early-life findings, KPI performance\n- External audit of assessment methodology (per OPINION_TEMPLATE.md)\n- Annual operational validation: actual system performance in production against predicted performance</p>\n<p><strong>Expected Evidence Level:</strong> L4–L5\n<strong>Engagement Duration:</strong> 12–16 weeks + 12-month post-deployment validation</p>\n<hr />"},{"h":"Evidence Catalogue Management","html":"<p><strong>Tools &amp; Systems:</strong>\n- Evidence repository: shared folder with access controls, version control, retention policy\n- Evidence tracking spreadsheet: category, item, status (collected/pending/gap), evidence level, tier applicability, claim mapping\n- Audit trail: document who accessed what evidence, when; maintains chain of custody for regulatory requirements</p>\n<p><strong>Annual Refresh:</strong>\nEvery December, review evidence catalogue to identify gaps or expired documentation. Update as systems evolve (new data source, model update, process change).</p>\n<hr />"}]};
